The fuzzer sends an IPv6 message. The IPv6 message payload is a CoAP request. The CoAP request has:
1. CoAP header (4 bytes minimum + token)
2. CoAP options (URI path "c/cg")
3. Payload marker (0xFF)
4. Payload containing MeshCoP TLVs

The vulnerability is in `HandleCommissioningGet`:
```cpp
void Leader::HandleCommissioningGet(Coap::Message &aMessage, const Ip6::MessageInfo &aMessageInfo)
{
    MeshCoP::Tlv tlv;
    uint16_t     offset = aMessage.GetOffset();
    uint8_t      tlvs[NetworkData::kMaxSize];  // 254 bytes
    uint8_t      length = 0;

    while (offset < aMessage.GetLength())
    {
        aMessage.Read(offset, sizeof(tlv), &tlv);

        if (tlv.GetType() == MeshCoP::Tlv::kGet)  // type == 13
        {
            length = tlv.GetLength();  // attacker-controlled, can be > 254
            aMessage.Read(offset + sizeof(MeshCoP::Tlv), length, tlvs);  // stack buffer overflow!
            break;
        }

        offset += sizeof(tlv) + tlv.GetLength();
    }

    SendCommissioningGetResponse(aMessage, aMessageInfo, tlvs, length);
}
```

The `tlv.GetLength()` returns a `uint8_t`, so the maximum value is 255. The buffer `tlvs` is 254 bytes. So if length is 255, we overflow by 1 byte. But actually, `aMessage.Read` reads `length` bytes into `tlvs`, and `length` is a `uint8_t` so max 255. The buffer is 254 bytes, so we overflow by 1 byte.

